\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Actor Union And Studios Start Talks On Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Sperling \par \uc2 481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The two sides announced the step a day after the leadership of the Hollywood writers' union approved a tentative deal that ended its monthslong strike.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood is moving one step closer to getting back to work.\par \par \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , the union that represents tens of thousands of movie and television actors who have been on strike since July, will resume negotiations with the major entertainment studios on Monday. Several studio executives will attend.\par \par \uc2 The joint announcement on Wednesday was made one day after the Writers Guild of America's leadership voted to end its 148-day strike against the studios. The tentative contract now goes to the union's membership for ratification, and writers began returning to work on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The flurry of movement is a welcome reprieve for an industry that has been hobbled for months by the dual strikes. (It was the first time the actors and the writers were both on strike since 1960.) TV and movie production has essentially been shut down, and fall prime-time and film release schedules have been upended.\par \par \uc2 But while late-night shows like ''The Late Show With Stephen Colbert'' and ''The Daily Show'' have announced that they will soon return, no scripted television or film projects can begin production again without a resolution to the actors' strike. If the strikes go on much longer, the 2024 summer moviegoing season will also be in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 The writers went on strike in May. On July 14, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , which represents more than 150,000 performers, joined them. There have been no talks between the actors' union and the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which bargains on behalf of the studios, for 76 days.\par \par \uc2 The tentative contract for the writers could offer a blueprint for the actors. Like the writers, the actors have been seeking guardrails around artificial intelligence and increased residuals (a type of royalty) for movies and television shows that appear on streaming services.\par \par \uc2 The writers' tentative contract guarantees that artificial intelligence technology will not encroach on writers' credits and compensation. And studios cannot use A.I. tools to rewrite original material. Studios will be able, however, to use film and TV scripts that they already own to refine A.I. tools and experiment.\par \par \uc2 Actors have been concerned about how A.I. could be used to create digital replicas of their likenesses without payment or approval.\par \par \uc2 The writers' contract also ensures, for the first time, a bonus from streaming services based on a percentage of active subscribers watching the shows. The actors have asked for a subscriber revenue-sharing agreement, a nonstarter for the studios.\par \par \uc2 The actors proposed an 11 percent raise in the first year of a new contract. The studios last offered a 5 percent raise.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230929ej9t00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
