\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 U.A.W. Says Auto Strike Could Grow\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  said the strikes against \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  could grow on Friday if negotiators don't make enough progress.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  said on Wednesday that it planned to expand its strike against the big three Michigan automakers on Friday if negotiators failed to make substantial progress on new contracts.\par \par \uc2 The union ordered workers to walk off the job nearly two weeks ago at three vehicle assembly plants -- each owned by one of the companies, \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent of Chrysler and \uc2 Jeep\uc2 . Last Friday the union broadened the strike to include spare parts-distribution centers owned by G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , saying it had made progress in its talks with Ford.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W. president, Shawn Fain, is scheduled to update members in a video streamed live on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 The union is seeking a substantial wage increase to make up for much smaller raises over the last decade. Each of the companies has offered to lift wages by roughly 20 percent over four years, about half of what the U.A.W. is seeking. The union has demanded other measures including cost-of-living adjustments, the right to strike to protest plant closures, pensions for more workers and company-paid health care for retirees.\par \par \uc2 The three plants that have been shut down by the strike include a G.M. factory in Wentzville, Mo., a Ford plant in Wayne, Mich., and a \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  complex in Toledo, Ohio. They make some of the manufacturers' most profitable models, including the GMC Canyon pickup truck, the Ford Bronco sport-utility vehicle, and the Jeep Wrangler.\par \par \uc2 The second wave of the strike idled 20 \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  parts-distribution centers and 18 owned by G.M. More than 18,000 U.A.W. workers are now on strike. The union represents about 150,000 workers employed by G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The union and the companies started negotiating new collective bargaining agreements in July, but made little progress until this month. Their contracts expired on Sept. 14 and Mr. Fain called on the first round of work stoppages the following day.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230928ej9s0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
