\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Actors' Equity Seeks to Unionize Production Assistants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Paulson \par \uc2 522 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The position is one of the few nonunion segments of the theater industry's work force.\par \par \uc2 Actors' Equity, the labor union representing American stage performers and stage managers, is seeking to unionize Broadway production assistants, one of the few nonunion segments of the industry work force.\par \par \uc2 The campaign comes at a moment when labor unions in the United States have become increasingly restive; there are organizing efforts in many sectors of the economy, and Hollywood's writers and actors have been on strike for months.\par \par \uc2 Broadway production assistants work with stage managers in entry-level positions that are usually filled only during rehearsal and preview periods. Equity described them as ''doing everything from preparing rehearsal materials to ensuring decisions made during rehearsals are recorded to being extra sets of hands and eyes during complicated technical rehearsals to efficiently running errands that keep the rehearsal productive.''\par \par \uc2 Many of the workers are young and are paid minimum wage, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Late on Thursday, Equity asked the Broadway League, a trade association representing producers, to voluntarily recognize \uc2 Actors' Equity Association\uc2  as the bargaining representative for production assistants working on commercial productions on Broadway and in sit-down productions, which are extended non-touring engagements produced by members of the Broadway League outside New York.\par \par \uc2 If the League does not agree, Equity said it would ask the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  to oversee an election.\par \par \uc2 ''Broadway is an extremely heavily unionized workplace, and these are some of the only folks without union contracts in these rooms,'' said Erin Maureen Koster, an Equity vice president who represents stage managers. Koster said that without union membership, production assistants have less protection should they be injured or harassed or have other concerns.\par \par \uc2 Equity said that there were only about a half-dozen people working in the job category on Broadway and in sit-down productions at any one time, but that about 100 people have worked in the position over the past two years. The union said the position was an important rung on the career ladder for people aspiring to work as stage managers on Broadway; even some people who have worked as stage managers Off Broadway or in regional theaters take temporary jobs as Broadway production assistants as a way to break into the industry.\par \par \uc2 ''As shows are getting more complicated, they are hiring more production assistants, and hiring qualified stage mangers into these roles,'' Stefanie Frey, Equity's director of organizing and mobilization, said. ''It's time.''\par \par \uc2 The Broadway League said in a statement that it was considering the union's request and looked forward to discussing it further. ''The Broadway League and our members support the right of employees to lawfully choose a bargaining representative,'' the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Actors' Equity says the production assistant position is an important rung on the career ladder for people aspiring to work as stage managers on Broadway. Most other jobs in the industry are unionized. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JUTHARAT PINYODOONYACHET FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page C2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230925ej9p00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
