\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Union Deal With Ford Could Put Added Pressure on Other 2 Detroit Automakers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 827 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Talks continued on Saturday with all three companies, a day after the U.A.W. expanded its strike at G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Detroit automakers and the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  continued to negotiate on Saturday, company representatives said, a day after the union expanded strikes in a way that could curtail the supply of spare parts for vehicles made by \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram.\par \par \uc2 U.A.W. members walked off the job at G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  parts distribution centers on Friday but spared Ford, saying the company had done more to meet its demands.\par \par \uc2 ''Our pressure on Ford is starting to pay off,'' the U.A.W. told members Saturday.\par \par \uc2 While there was no indication a deal with Ford was imminent, an agreement with the company could put pressure on the other two to offer similar terms and lead to a speedy end to the strike, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 ''The moment you get a deal with Ford that includes much or all of what the U.A.W. is looking for, that puts a lot of pressure on G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ,'' said Michael Duff, a professor at the Saint Louis University School of Law and a former attorney for the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . ''They are putting them in a position of having to argue why they're different, why they can't give anything more.''\par \par \uc2 A short strike would be good news for the economy. About 200,000 people work in auto manufacturing, according to the Alliance for Automotive Innovation, an industry group. That figure does not include jobs that are indirectly dependent on car making, which is several times higher.\par \par \uc2 Lost wages would hurt consumer spending, while inflation could rise if some vehicles become hard to get, or repair shops and dealerships run short of spare parts. If so, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would have to continue its efforts to slow the economy by keeping official interest rates high. More than 18,000 U.A.W. members are now on strike.\par \par \uc2 The auto companies also face pressure from public opinion. The autoworkers' argument that their wages have not kept up with inflation, while carmakers have reported healthy profits, resonates with the public, said Ivana Delevska, founder of Spear Invest, an investment firm.\par \par \uc2 ''Inflation is up across the board. They need to pay their cost of living,'' Ms. Delevska said of workers.\par \par \uc2 Polls show that the workers have public opinion on their side, but that could shift if a long strike makes it hard for people to get their cars fixed or is perceived as damaging the economy, Mr. Duff said. ''As the strike drags on, you can have disillusionment with workers,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  workers walked out at 20 of the company's parts distribution centers Friday, while G.M. workers went on strike at 18 centers.\par \par \uc2 A deal that Ford reached this past week with the union that represents its Canadian workers could offer clues to the outcome in the United States. The deal with Unifor provides for pay increases worth up to 25 percent over the three years of the contract, as well as bonuses, improved retirement benefits and measures to protect employees as Ford retools factories for electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Unifor, which probably has less leverage than the U.A.W. because Ford has a much smaller presence in Canada, achieved those gains without having to walk out. The union is negotiating separately with G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Investors are expecting the carmakers and the unions in America to agree on a wage increase of less than 30 percent, Ms. Delevska said, who added that both sides have an incentive to settle quickly. ''It's in nobody's best interest that this extends much longer,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W.'s demands include a 40 percent wage increase over four years, improved retiree benefits and shorter work hours. The union also wants an end to a tiered wage system that starts new hires at much lower wages than the top U.A.W. pay of $32 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Ford agreed to some of the U.A.W.'s demands, according to the union, for example promising to adjust workers' pay in step with inflation and increase their profit-sharing bonuses.\par \par \uc2 Ford also agreed to give workers the right to strike over plant closings, an important concession. The union is worried that carmakers will shutter some factories as the industry shifts to electric vehicles, which require fewer parts and labor.\par \par \uc2 ''These are historic gains,'' the U.A.W. said Saturday in a message to members, ''but we have further to go.''\par \par \uc2 Striking members of the U.A.W. on Friday outside a General Motors parts center in Burton, Mich. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK HAGEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230924ej9o00063\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
