\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Progress in Hollywood Writers' Strike Negotiations, but No Deal Yet\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brooks Barnes and John Koblin \par \uc2 812 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A third straight day of bargaining between the studios and the union ended without an agreement. Talks will continue on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 A third straight day of marathon negotiations between Hollywood studios and striking screenwriters ended on Friday night without a deal. But the sides made substantial progress, according to three people briefed on the talks.\par \par \uc2 The sides plan to reconvene on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The Friday session started at 11 a.m. Pacific time at the suburban Los Angeles headquarters of the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which bargains on behalf of the major entertainment companies. For the third day in a row, several Hollywood moguls directly participated in the negotiations, which ended a little after 8 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Robert A. Iger\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 's chief executive; \uc2 Donna Langley\uc2 , \uc2 NBCUniversal\uc2 's chief content officer of \uc2 Universal Pictures\uc2 ; \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 , co-chief executive of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 ; and \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  had previously delegated bargaining with the union to others. Their direct involvement -- which many screenwriters and some analysts said was long overdue -- contributed to meaningful progress over the past few days, according to the people familiar with the talks, who spoke on condition of anonymity because of the diplomatic nature of the efforts.\par \par \uc2 During the Thursday negotiations, the sides had narrowed their differences, for instance, on the topic of minimum staffing for television show writers' rooms, a point that studios had been unwilling to engage on before the guild called a strike in early May.\par \par \uc2 The Thursday session took a turn, however, after the sides agreed to take a short break at roughly 5 p.m., according to the people familiar with the talks. The executives and studio labor lawyers had expected guild negotiators to return to discuss points they had been working on earlier. Instead, the guild made additional requests -- one being that a return to work by screenwriters be tied to a resolution of the actors' strike.\par \par \uc2 The actors' union, known as \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , joined writers on picket lines on July 14. Its demands exceed those of the Writers Guild. Among other things, the actors want 2 percent of the total revenue generated by streaming shows, something that studios have said is a nonstarter.\par \par \uc2 Several hours after talks ended on Thursday night, the guild emailed its membership to say that the sides would meet on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''Your negotiating committee appreciates all the messages of solidarity and support we have received the last few days, and ask as many of you as possible to come out to the picket lines tomorrow,'' the email said.\par \par \uc2 The guild extended picketing hours on Friday to 2 p.m. Pickets have typically ended at noon.\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles, several hundred writers turned up to picket outside the arching \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2  gate, far more than in recent weeks. The Writers Guild and \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  have been staging themed pickets to keep members engaged, and the theme on Friday happened to be ''puppet day,'' meaning that, in addition to picket signs, some marchers held felt hand puppets and marionettes. The mood was optimistic.\par \par \uc2 Outside \uc2 Netflix\uc2 's Hollywood offices on Friday afternoon, picketing writers even began offering goodbye speeches, delivered via bullhorn. At the \uc2 CBS\uc2  lot in Studio City, the theme was ''silent disco,'' with several hundred writers dance-picketing while wearing headphones.\par \par \uc2 The talks were mostly back on track by the time picketing ended on Friday, according to two of the people familiar with the matter. On the sticky issue of minimum staffing for television shows, the sides were discussing a proposal in which at least four writers would be hired regardless of the number of episodes or whether a showrunner felt that the work could be done with fewer. (Earlier in the week, studios were pushing for a sliding number based on the number of episodes.)\par \par \uc2 They were also discussing a plan in which writers would for the first time receive payments from streaming services -- in addition to other fees -- based on a percentage of active subscribers. The guild had originally asked the entertainment companies to establish a viewership-based royalty payment (known in Hollywood as a residual) to ''reward programs with greater viewership.''\par \par \uc2 The writers have been on strike for 144 days. The longest writers' strike was 153 days in 1988.\par \par \uc2 ''Thank you for the wonderful show of support on the picket lines today!'' the guild's negotiating committee said in an email to members late Friday. ''It means so much to us as we continue to work toward a deal that writers deserve.''\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sperling contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nicole Sperling contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Dual strikes by writers and actors have brought much of the entertainment industry to a standstill. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Jenna Schoenefeld for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230923ej9n0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
