\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Big 3 Strike Offers \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Opportunity And Pitfalls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jack Ewing \par \uc2 1,557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  chief executive, may be able to exploit his rivals' weaknesses, but the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2  also has the electric carmaker in its sights.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2  strike against the Michigan automakers would seem to be nothing but good news for \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , the electric vehicle maker that has upended the industry and stolen customers from \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram.\par \par \uc2 Unencumbered by an activist union, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  can take advantage of the work stoppages to add to its substantial lead in battery technology and software. As the three established automakers face increases in labor costs and struggle to master electric vehicles, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  can twist the knife by lowering car prices because it is much more profitable than most automakers.\par \par \uc2 But the U.A.W.'s determination to secure a big victory for its members, amid a nationwide resurgence in union activism, harbors risks for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , its chief executive, who has attacked and ridiculed unions on his social media network, X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.A.W., which has failed to organize \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's factory workers in the past, is gearing up for another attempt, a top union official said.\par \par \uc2 ''There is a group of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  workers who are actively talking about forming a union and creating the best representation they can for themselves and their co-workers through collective bargaining,'' said Mike Miller, the director of the U.A.W.'s Region 6, which includes California and Nevada, where \uc2 Tesla\uc2  makes cars and batteries. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  also has a large factory in Austin, Texas, not too far from a unionized G.M. factory in the Dallas-Fort Worth area.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Mr. Miller declined to provide more details or identify the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  workers, saying they needed time to prepare before going public. This union organizing effort is separate from a campaign at a Buffalo plant where \uc2 Tesla\uc2  makes electric vehicle chargers and employs data entry workers.\par \par \uc2 But as representatives of the national union demand 40 percent wage increases from the Detroit automakers, along with significant gains in benefits, they are certainly thinking about the signal that any deal would send to nonunion workers at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Clearly the narrative out there is that this can't be good for the Big Three, and if it's not good for the Big Three, it's good for \uc2 Tesla\uc2 ,'' said Rahul Kapoor, a professor of management at the \uc2 Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he added, ''If I'm an autoworker with wages lower than what Ford and G.M. are paying, and I hear there is a substantial increase, it's very likely I would want to take that into account.''\par \par \uc2 The president of the U.A.W., Shawn Fain, fired a warning shot at Mr. Musk Sunday on CBS News's ''Face the Nation.''\par \par \uc2 ''Most of these workers in those companies are scraping to get by so that greedy C.E.O.s and greedy people like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  can build more rocket ships and shoot theirself in outer space,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A lot has changed since 2016, when a group of workers at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's auto assembly plant in Fremont, Calif., began an organizing drive that never acquired enough momentum to come to a vote.\par \par \uc2 Back then, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  was a struggling upstart flirting with bankruptcy. Now, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  dominates the market for electric vehicles, with a 60 percent share in the United States. It is worth vastly more on the stock market than the three established U.S. automakers combined. It is arguably in a better position to reward workers than its rivals.\par \par \uc2 Yet labor organizing is arduous. Activists must get at least 30 percent of workers to sign union cards and force a vote overseen by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . Companies often do all they can to dissuade workers from joining, hiring lawyers and consultants who specialize in defeating union campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Even if a majority of workers cast ballots in favor of a union, winning pay increases and better benefits comes only after negotiations that can drag for years. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers at a Staten Island warehouse voted in April 2022 to unionize, but \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has challenged the result and has yet to begin bargaining on a contract.\par \par \uc2 Still, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  would be a tempting target for unions. The company reported profit of $2.7 billion on sales of $25 billion in the second quarter, giving it a profit margin of about 11 percent. That profit margin is more than that of Ford or G.M., even after an exceptionally profitable period for those companies. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which was created by the 2021 merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  and \uc2 Peugeot\uc2 , reported an 11 percent profit margin in the first six months of the year, but lost market share in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's stronger financial performance has allowed it to significantly cut car prices, making it harder for the established carmakers to gain ground in electric vehicles. The least expensive Model 3 sedan costs about $33,000 after federal tax credits, less than comparable gasoline vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The climate for organized labor is better than it has been for years. President Biden is a big supporter of unions. Hollywood writers and actors are on strike, a high-profile manifestation of labor activism. In August, \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  employees won their biggest raises ever in a contract negotiated by the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment, but Mr. Musk seemed to acknowledge the union threat last week, saying on X that his workers were better off than employees of G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . ''We pay more than the U.A.W.,'' he said, although he added that ''performance expectations are also higher.''\par \par \uc2 The traditional automakers quarreled with Mr. Musk's math, saying that they pay their workers substantially more and that a big raise would only widen the gap and undermine their ability to invest in electric vehicle and battery factories. Ford says its hourly employees make an average of $112,000 per year including benefits, compared with about $90,000 at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Ford figures do not include stock options that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  grants employees, and that can be worth a lot. Mr. Musk has said that some production workers have become millionaires from their shares in the company.\par \par \uc2 Stock options can be lucrative but also risky. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has not detailed how often or in what amount it distributes options to rank-and-file workers. In regulatory filings, the company has said the options that those workers receive have a vesting period, meaning that employees must remain at the company for a certain period to cash them in.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  workers may lose their options if they are fired or forced to quit because of injury or poor health, said Bryan Schwartz, a lawyer in Oakland, Calif., who has represented the company's employees in lawsuits against the company.\par \par \uc2 ''There are lots of issues with options to the degree workers can really count on them,'' Mr. Schwartz said.\par \par \uc2 Stock awards fluctuate in value along with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's share price. The stock peaked at more than $400 in late 2021, plummeted to a little more than $100 last year and rebounded this year to $270. The uncertainty may be unsettling for workers trying to make mortgage payments and pay for child care.\par \par \uc2 ''If I was a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  worker, with all these other companies making E.V.s, I would prefer a wage,'' said Rick Eckstein, a professor of sociology at \uc2 Villanova University\uc2  who follows labor issues.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has a reputation as a tough place to work, with long hours and punishing deadlines. Mr. Schwartz has sued \uc2 Tesla\uc2  on behalf of Black employees who say they faced discrimination in promotions and work assignments and were subject to racist abuse. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has denied the accusations.\par \par \uc2 Any union drive would face forceful opposition from Mr. Musk. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has found that Mr. Musk illegally threatened employees in 2018 by implying they would lose their stock options if they voted to unionize. The labor board also found that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  illegally fired one of the lead organizers.\par \par \uc2 An appeals court upheld the board's decision. \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which argues that Mr. Musk and the company did nothing wrong, is appealing the court ruling.\par \par \uc2 Without doubt, the strike poses huge risks for the Detroit automakers, which were slow to take \uc2 Tesla\uc2  seriously and stand to lose precious time they need to catch up.\par \par \uc2 ''The real winner in the U.A.W. strike will likely be the auto company that has been winning all along,'' Gary Black, managing partner of the \uc2 Future Fund\uc2 , an investment firm that owns \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock, said on X.\par \par \uc2 But any schadenfreude among \uc2 Tesla\uc2  investors could be brief.\par \par \uc2 ''The strike could be a bellwether,'' said Mr. Eckstein of Villanova. ''It's a hot time in the labor movement.''\par \par \uc2 Above, members of the United Automobile Workers protesting outside the Stellantis headquarters in Auburn Hills, Mich. Left, a delivery of Tesla electric vehicles. With tax credits, many Teslas cost less than comparable gas vehicles. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY NICK HAGEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; JIM WILSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B3) This article appeared in print on page B1, B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230922ej9m0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
