\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Ford Averts Strike in Canada As Talks in U.S. Inch Along\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 1,184 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  is threatening to expand strikes on Friday if it does not make significant progress in talks with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Negotiations between each of the three large U.S. automakers and the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  remain far from being resolved, but one of the companies -- \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  -- has averted a second strike in Canada.\par \par \uc2 Late on Tuesday, the company reached a tentative labor agreement with \uc2 Unifor\uc2 , Canada's main auto union. The deal was announced minutes before an 11:59 p.m. deadline set by the union for a strike by its 5,600 members at Ford.\par \par \uc2 Neither side disclosed the terms of the agreement, but \uc2 Unifor\uc2  said the company had made a ''substantive offer.''\par \par \uc2 ''We believe that this agreement will solidify the foundations on which we will continue to bargain gains for generations of autoworkers in Canada,'' \uc2 Unifor\uc2 's national president, Lana Payne, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Unifor\uc2 's talks with Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram, started on Aug. 10 but have been overshadowed by the U.A.W. contract talks in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Ford has an assembly plant and two engine plants in Canada. \uc2 Unifor\uc2  selected Ford as the ''target'' of its talks, meaning it focused on securing the best deal it could from the company before turning to the other two automakers. Now, it will seek to strike similar agreements with G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ford's deal in Canada appears to have little bearing on the U.A.W. strikes in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Last Thursday, the U.A.W. told nearly 13,000 workers to leave their jobs at three U.S. plants: a G.M. pickup truck factory in Wentzville, Mo.; a Ford truck and sport utility vehicle plant in Wayne, Mich.; and a Stellantis S.U.V. plant in Toledo, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 The talks appear to have progressed only a little since the strikes began on Friday. On Wednesday, the U.A.W. said it was reviewing a new offer from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  but declined to provide details.\par \par \uc2 The union is seeking a 40 percent increase in wages over four years, saying the pay of the automakers' chief executives rose by roughly that much over the previous four years. The companies have offered raises of just over 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. union also wants more workers to qualify for pension plans, company-paid health care for retirees, shorter working hours and other improvements. And the U.A.W. is seeking an end to a practice under which new hires are paid about $17 an hour -- a bit more than half the top union wage of $32 an hour.\par \par \uc2 At $32 an hour, a U.A.W. member working 40 hours a week is paid about $67,000 a year. In recent years, the companies have paid workers profit-sharing bonuses of $9,000 to $15,000.\par \par \uc2 Outside \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's North American headquarters in Auburn Hills, Mich., on Wednesday, workers who are not on strike picketed in support of the work stoppage, chanting, ''No justice, no Jeeps.''\par \par \uc2 Josh Boyd, 36, an auto mechanic who works at the headquarters' technical center, said he was ready to walk out if asked by the union. ''There's always uncertainty, but there's also excitement,'' he said. ''I think we're going to get a good contract.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Boyd, who carried his young daughter on his shoulder, said that he earned $32 an hour, but that his family of three was stretched. ''Day care is $250 a week,'' he said. ''I've got a mortgage. My wife is in school, so we are on one income.''\par \par \uc2 LaShawn English, who was elected this year as the director of the U.A.W.'s Region 1, which includes parts of Michigan and Canada, said the wage increases offered by the automakers would apply to most but not all workers. Among those who would not get the same raises are temporary workers who make up about 12 percent of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's unionized work force of 43,000.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not just about the higher-wage workers,'' she said. ''We have to move everybody forward. We can't leave people behind.''\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Wednesday, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  presented a new offer to the union but did not disclose details other than to say it primarily addressed issues other than wages. The company also said it had to lay off 68 workers at a machining plant in Ohio, and might have to lay off 300 more in Indiana because of the U.A.W.'s strike at its Toledo plant, which makes Jeeps.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the U.A.W. president, Shawn Fain, said the union might expand the strike to additional plants this week if it did not make significant progress toward an agreement. Mr. Fain is expected to announce additional strike locations Friday morning with workers leaving their jobs at noon.\par \par \uc2 In the past, the U.A.W. typically struck at all locations of one automaker at a time. Mr. Fain was elected president of the union this year on promises to take a more combative approach. His unusual strike strategy, frequent media appearances and strident criticisms of management appear to have caught the automakers off guard.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Mr. Fain appeared at a rally of several hundred workers in Detroit along with Senator Bernie Sanders, the Vermont independent.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, G.M.'s second-highest-ranking executive, its president, \uc2 Mark Reuss\uc2 , sought to rebut Mr. Fain's criticisms in an opinion essay in The Detroit Free Press.\par \par \uc2 He said G.M. had offered to increase wages 20 percent over the next four years, which would lift the top wage to more than $39 an hour, or about $82,000 a year, based on a 40-hour workweek. Entry-level workers now earning $17 an hour would reach $39 an hour after four years.\par \par \uc2 ''U.A.W. leadership claims G.M. pays its team members 'poverty' wages,''' Mr. Reuss wrote. ''This is simply not true.''\par \par \uc2 While G.M. is making near-record profits -- it made almost $10 billion in 2022 -- Mr. Reuss said the company was investing heavily to make the transition to electric vehicles, including $11 billion this year. He added that the company could not afford to pay what the U.A.W. was seeking if it wanted to remain competitive and healthy.\par \par \uc2 ''The fundamental reality is that the U.A.W.'s demands can be described in one word -- untenable,'' he wrote, adding, ''As the past has clearly shown, nobody wins in a strike.''\par \par \uc2 Separately, the U.A.W. said on Wednesday that 190 union members went on strike at a Tuscaloosa, Ala., plant owned by ZF, a company that supplies axles to \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A protest Wednesday at Stellantis headquarters in Auburn Hills, Mich. Talks between automakers and union officials are moving slowly. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK HAGEN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230921ej9l0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
