\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Scott Takes Harsh Line Against Auto Strikers, Citing Reagan's Dictum\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Astor \par \uc2 713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mr. Scott, when asked if he would involve himself in the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  dispute as president, took a harsher stance than many other Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina took a harsher stance toward striking autoworkers on Monday than many of his fellow Republican presidential candidates, saying it did not make sense for workers to want higher pay for shorter workweeks and noting approvingly that President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  had fired federal employees for striking.\par \par \uc2 ''I think \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  gave us a great example when federal employees decided they were going to strike,'' Mr. Scott said at a campaign event in Iowa, in response to a voter who had asked whether he would ''insert'' himself into the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  talks as president. ''He said, 'You strike, you're fired.' Simple concept to me, to the extent that we can use that once again.''\par \par \uc2 He went on to criticize federal funding for private-sector unions' pension plans and said with regard to the U.A.W. dispute: ''The other things that are really important in that deal is that they want more money working fewer hours. They want more benefits working fewer days.''\par \par \uc2 In ''America, that doesn't make sense,'' he said. ''That's not common sense.''\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  went on a targeted strike on Friday against three automakers: \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The workers are seeking raises of up to 40 percent -- which would correspond with pay increases for their companies' executives over the past decade -- and four-day workweeks, along with cost-of-living adjustments and a restoration of previously forfeited pensions.\par \par \uc2 Despite Mr. Scott's approving reference to Reagan, who fired thousands of striking air traffic controllers in 1981, \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  members are not federal employees and cannot be fired by the president. Federal labor law also protects them from being fired by their employers for striking.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Scott's campaign emphasized the rest of his answer -- his rejection of taxpayer funding for any deal -- and said the Reagan portion had referred to federal workers, not the U.A.W. But it declined to respond on the record when asked why he had brought up Reagan's firing of federal workers if that had no relevance to the U.A.W. dispute.\par \par \uc2 ''Senator Scott has repeatedly made clear, both at that event and others, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  shouldn't leave taxpayers on the hook for any labor deal,'' a campaign spokesman, Matt Gorman, said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Scott's criticism of the workers' demands set him apart from many other Republican candidates who have commented on the U.A.W. strike, though not all of them have weighed in. While most of the other candidates have been critical of unions in general, with particular vitriol for teachers' unions, they have generally expressed sympathy with the autoworkers' economic concerns.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  is actively trying to court the striking workers while denouncing their leaders; he has cast the workers as victims of Biden administration rules intended to ensure that two-thirds of new passenger cars sold in the United States are electric by 2032.\par \par \uc2 Three other candidates, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  of North Dakota, have also brought up the push for electric vehicles in offering sympathy, directly or obliquely, for the workers. Mr. Pence has emphasized inflation, too, while denying that the growing gap between workers' and executives' salaries is a factor -- though the workers themselves have cited it.\par \par \uc2 One exception to the trend was Nikki Haley, the former governor of South Carolina, who has proudly described herself as a ''union buster.'' She told Fox News after the U.A.W. strike began that it would increase auto prices and hurt consumers, and that President Biden was to blame because his support had encouraged unions to make unreasonable demands.\par \par \uc2 ''When you have 'the most pro-union president' and he touts that he is emboldening the unions, this is what you get,'' she said. ''And I'll tell you who pays for it is the taxpayers.''\par \par \uc2 Tim Scott This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230920ej9k0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
