\par\par \uc2 PETER COY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In This Strike, Autoworkers Have the Higher Ground\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Coy \par \uc2 1,902 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  have a strong hand in their strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . Their negotiators have reason to believe that they should come close to getting what they're asking for, which includes raises of 40 percent over four years. I don't foresee the autoworkers caving. If the automakers also refuse to cave, this strike could drag on, harming both parties to the conflict as well as the general public. That would be unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the U.A.W. struck all three companies at once for the first time. It began what it called a ''stand up strike,'' picketing just three assembly plants, one from each of the companies. It's likely the targets will change. The idea is to throw the automakers off balance while allowing most union members to keep drawing a paycheck. ''As time goes on, more locals may be called on to 'Stand Up' and join the strike,'' the union said.\par \par \uc2 In a contract negotiation such as this involving powerful parties that need each other, there is no one clearly correct outcome. Both labor and management gain immensely from their partnership. The fight is over how to divide the value that they jointly create. It would seem unfair for either the companies or the workers to extract 100 percent of it. But what's the right split? Is it 50-50? And how would you measure such a split, anyway?\par \par \uc2 In the absence of an undisputed standard, the U.A.W. and the automakers are using different yardsticks to make their cases. It seems to me that the U.A.W. is making a more appealing one. It also helps them that big business in general is not held in high esteem these days. The chart below shows that since at least the early 1970s, there has never been a bigger gap between the public's confidence in organized labor (which is so-so) and its confidence in big business (which is abysmal).\par \par \uc2 ''In modern corporate America the people who don't do the work get paid many times more than the people who do the work, so picking sides in these disputes is generally not difficult,'' Hamilton Nolan, a journalist who writes about labor and politics, wrote this week on \uc2 Substack\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The public doesn't feel the same warmth toward autoworkers that it feels toward striking actors or the U.P.S. drivers they know, who nearly went on strike this summer, but the autoworkers' dispute revolves around ''issues that resonate with the public, the same issues that they're dealing with,'' Kate Bronfenbrenner, the director of labor education research at Cornell University's School of Industrial and Labor Relations, told me.\par \par \uc2 The first thing that tends to affect the public in an auto strike is that replacement parts run low. People get angry when they can't get to work or the supermarket because their cars can't be repaired. When that happens, ''they're going to blame the company, not the union,'' Bronfenbrenner predicted. Some angry customers will switch to other makes of cars -- which is ironic, because those other companies (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  in particular) generally offer workers a worse deal.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that gives the autoworkers a strong hand is that the automakers can't go for very long without running out of vehicles to sell. As the chart below shows, the seasonally adjusted ratio of inventories to sales, at less than 1.0, is far below its 30-year average of around 2.5. This strike will be a war of attrition between the autoworkers' steadily dwindling strike fund, which pays workers while they're on the picket line, and the automakers' steadily dwindling stock of vehicles for sale.\par \par \uc2 I recently watched a replay of an Aug. 1 \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Live event hosted by Shawn Fain, the fiery U.A.W. president. Fain, who was elected in March, was wearing a short-sleeved black shirt bearing the U.A.W. logo and sitting in front of some easels with charts. He gave folksy shout-outs to union members tuning in from across the country, including Kokomo, Ind., where he got his start in 1994 as an electrician at Chrysler's Kokomo Casting Plant. (Chrysler is now part of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which is headquartered in the Netherlands.)\par \par \uc2 You wouldn't expect someone in Fain's position to say that the union's requests were ''audacious,'' but that's what he did. I think he was trying to tell his members that they were entitled to ask for more to make up for a long time when they got very little. When the financial crisis of 2007-9 jeopardized the automakers, the union agreed to give up its annual cost-of-living adjustments, which had begun in 1948. It also accepted expanded use of a two-tier wage structure. Today the starting wage is $18.04 an hour, which is lower than the 2007 starting wage of $19.60, Fain said. If the starting wage had kept up with inflation it would be around $29 today, he noted.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile the automakers rebounded from the financial crisis and began to make a lot of money -- $250 billion from 2013 through 2022, according to the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 . G.M. and Chrysler were prohibited from issuing big dividends or stock buybacks to reward shareholders as a condition of the federal bailouts they received, but soon after the government sold the last of its shares (in 2011 for Chrysler and 2013 for G.M.), they resumed big payouts in earnest. They also boosted top executives' pay.\par \par \uc2 The automakers have argued in negotiations that they will need lots of money to make the transition to electric vehicles, but the big payouts to shareholders and executives somewhat undercut them.\par \par \uc2 ''The companies made mistakes,'' Bronfenbrenner said. ''They underestimated the depth of the anger and the depth of the organization, how well the unions are prepared. They haven't dealt with this kind of organization in the union for a long time.''\par \par \uc2 ''There's a lot of bitterness that I don't think we've seen for a very long time,'' Garrett Nelson, a senior equity research analyst for CFRA, told me this week. He said that among expenses for automakers, labor is a distant second to the cost of materials, such as steel. ''So they do have some room'' to pay more, he said. ''It is digestible.''\par \par \uc2 Another analyst, \uc2 Daniel Ives\uc2  of \uc2 Wedbush Securities\uc2 , disagreed, saying that big pay increases would ''make the Detroit Three put one hand behind their back in a fist fight against \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .'' He said it would be impossible for the companies to give the U.A.W. the raises it wants and ''keep the same profit margin that they've promised to the Street.''\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  does enjoy an unfair labor-cost advantage, which the union will certainly work to eliminate by organizing \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees. As far as helping the automakers keep their promises to Wall Street, that is not high on the U.A.W.'s priority list.\par \par \uc2 I do understand that autoworkers at G.M. and what's now \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  got preferential treatment, compared with other parties to whom the automakers owed money, in the Obama administration's bailout of those two companies in 2009. Autoworkers at the three companies remain among the best-paid factory workers in America. I also understand that the chiefs of the Detroit Three don't want to lock themselves into expensive, inflexible contracts that will leave them exposed to failure in the next big downturn.\par \par \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , the chief executive of Ford, said this week that Ford's latest offer is the most generous in 80 years. He said it gives pay increases, eliminates tiering of wages, offers protection against inflation and increases contributions to retirement plans. He added that he was ''still optimistic that we'll get a deal, but there is a limit.'' Unstated was the possibility that the automakers could shut down U.S. factories and move the jobs to cheaper places.\par \par \uc2 On the whole, though, the autoworkers do seem to have the stronger argument, and the stronger hand, as this strike begins.\par \par \uc2 The Readers Write\par \par \uc2 The authors of ''Superabundance'' remind us of the economists' curse to know the price of everything and the value of nothing. Most environmentalists I know and work with embrace innovation and change that advance the human -- and planetary -- condition. What we insist on is measuring the real immediate and long-term inclusive costs of goods and services, including ''externalized'' environmental and social costs that are not reflected in current market prices, with appropriate discount rates that value the human condition of our children and grandchildren, not just ourselves.\par \par \uc2 Angus Duncan\par \par \uc2 Portland, Ore.\par \par \uc2 The writer is a consultant to the \uc2 Natural Resources Defense Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Resources are not inexhaustible. The rapid extraction of water from deep, ancient water tables in America's High Plains, the contamination of water with pesticides and other waste-chemical streams, and the destruction of natural ecosystems that help purify water and store carbon are all threatening our civilizations.\par \par \uc2 Mark Silverstone\par \par \uc2 Stavanger, Norway\par \par \uc2 I wonder if there's any way to incorporate an anxiety index into poverty measures. Many people today feel that they are at the intersection of two tight, narrow, walled streets, one named Do Not Enter and the other named Dead End. True, a certain amount of anxiety may be productive, but when anxiety gives way to despair, depression, or resignation it is not good for society. I am open to arguments that some people like it that way, as it makes it easier to hire cheap and malleable workers.\par \par \uc2 Mark Hull\par \par \uc2 Missoula, Mont.\par \par \uc2 I knew the computer-aided-design industry and many of its top executives in the 1990s. This industry originated in Defense Department funding at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2  in the 1950s. By the 1970s there was a thriving commercial industry. The United States has a firm monopoly grip on CAD software for computer chips today. As another example, the high-resolution film for photolithography in semiconductor manufacturing came out of spy satellite research. In many cases, government spending and encouragement reduces the risk for entrepreneurs. Then capitalism takes over.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Whitney\par \par \uc2 Redmond, Wash.\par \par \uc2 Quote of the Day\par \par \uc2 ''What really hooked me on the data was actually I started working on data collection for an organization that, at the time, was called Data Resources. I started when I was in high school. And I started copying numbers out of big books, historical numbers for housing prices in the library, Baker Library at Harvard. I went down to the basement. And Data Resources, as I say, I started working with them when I was in high school because I used to babysit for the vice president and then he graduated me from babysitting to data entry.''\par \par \uc2 -- Catherine Mann, then global chief economist of Citibank, in a podcast interview with the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis\uc2  (June 16, 2021)\par \par \uc2 A Note on Reader Mail\par \par \uc2 Many thanks to all of you who write with praise, criticism and ideas. While I can't respond to every email, I do read all of them. Please keep writing!\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ALEXEY MOROZOV D CSA IMAGES/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page A22. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230918ej9i00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
