\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Strike by Auto Workers Could Have Wide Economic Consequences\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 769 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An extended walkout by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  in a contract dispute could raise car prices and affect jobs at the companies' suppliers.\par \par \uc2 Two years after the auto industry survived the supply-chain upheaval of the pandemic, another disruption -- the prospective strike by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  -- threatens to upend the production and distribution of new cars, and the impact could be wide-ranging.\par \par \uc2 A U.A.W. strike against one or more of Detroit's Big Three -- \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns Chrysler, \uc2 Jeep\uc2  and Ram -- is likely to quickly affect the U.S. economy, particularly in the Midwest. And a prolonged strike, by crimping the availability of new vehicles, could lead to soaring car prices. The combination of slower growth and higher prices could complicate matters for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which has sought to bring down inflation while maintaining job growth.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been counting on vehicle prices coming down, adding to the disinflation and taking pressure off the Fed so the Fed doesn't have to keep on raising interest rates,'' said \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. ''This makes that much more difficult.''\par \par \uc2 According to an August report from the Anderson Economic Group, a 10-day strike against all three automakers would result in total economic losses of $5.6 billion. Around $3.5 billion of that would result from lost wages and production, with the remaining $2.1 billion borne by consumers, who wouldn't be able to get necessary repairs and replacement parts, and by dealers and their employees.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zandi said a six-week strike would have a ''measurable but ultimately modest'' effect on overall gross domestic product, perhaps a decline of two- or three-tenths of a percentage point. But he said damage would start to mount, given economic headwinds like rising interest rates, the return of student-loan repayments and a potential government shutdown in October.\par \par \uc2 If the strike lasted through the end of the year, Mr. Zandi said, ''that would be enough to push this economy close to the edge of a recession, given everything else that's going on.''\par \par \uc2 A 40-day strike against \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in 2019 had limited economic effects. One key difference this time is inventories. Total domestic car inventories, which includes new and used cars, have increased from a record low in February 2022 but are less than a quarter of what they were in September 2019.\par \par \uc2 ''In 2019, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  could look at their inventory and say, 'We can take a 10-day strike, and hardly anybody who wants one of our cars is going to be unable to get it,'' said \uc2 Patrick Anderson\uc2 , the principal and chief executive of the Anderson Economic Group. ''That's not the case in 2023.''\par \par \uc2 A strike could also have a spillover effect on the automotive supply chain. Gabriel Ehrlich, an economic forecaster at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , said the automakers' suppliers -- the businesses that make brakes, headlights and catalytic converters -- would begin to be felt after about two weeks, with employers cutting back on employment and, as a result, those laid-off workers reducing their own spending.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, the auto industry has slipped in prominence but still contributes meaningfully to the economy. Mr. Ehrlich's analysis, which assumes a six-week strike against just one automaker, forecasts a slowdown in payroll growth in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 How the individual automakers weather the storm could vary. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  will be able to satisfy consumer demand longer than Ford or \uc2 General Motors\uc2  because it has greater inventories, according to Pat Ryan, the chief executive of Co-Pilot, a car-shopping app that tracks the inventories of car dealers. The result will still be higher prices for consumers, Mr. Ryan said, for both used and new vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the automakers will be able to make up for lost production, and selling their vehicles at higher prices -- in addition to not paying wages during the strike -- will help for a time. But things will become more challenging if automakers are forced to stop making their most profitable and popular cars, which are already in short supply.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're a G.M. dealer or G.M., you're going to feel a lot of pain if the Tahoe line shuts down,'' Mr. Ryan said.\par \par \uc2 A strike against one or more of Detroit's Big Three is likely to quickly affect the U.S. economy, particularly in the Midwest.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Mike Householder/Associated Press FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230914ej9e0007h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
