\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Unions and Restaurants In California Reach Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal will avoid a ballot fight over a law passed last year that could have resulted in higher pay and other changes opposed by restaurant companies and franchisees.\par \par \uc2 Labor groups and fast-food companies in California have reached an agreement that will pave the way for workers in the industry to receive a minimum wage of $20 per hour.\par \par \uc2 The deal, which will result in changes to Assembly Bill 1228, was announced by the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  on Monday, and will mean an increase to the minimum wage for California fast-food workers by April. In exchange, labor groups and their allies in the Legislature will agree to the fast-food industry's demands to remove a provision from the bill that could have made restaurant companies liable for workplace violations committed by their franchisees.\par \par \uc2 The agreement is contingent on the withdrawal of a referendum proposal by restaurant companies in California that would have challenged the proposed legislation in the 2024 ballot. Businesses, labor groups and others have often used ballot measures in California to block legislation or advance their causes. The proposed legislation would also create a council for overseeing future increases to the minimum wage and enact workplace regulations.\par \par \uc2 Mary Kay Henry\uc2 , the president of the S.E.I.U., said the measure in California would be a model for other states. ''California fast-food workers' fight for a seat at the table has reshaped what working people believe is possible when they join together,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Sean Kennedy, the executive vice president of public affairs at the \uc2 National Restaurant Association\uc2 , said the deal also benefited restaurants. ''This agreement protects local restaurant owners from significant threats that would have made it difficult to continue to operate in California,'' he said. ''It provides a more predictable and stable future for restaurants, workers and consumers.''\par \par \uc2 Even so, some franchisees said they did not support the deal.\par \par \uc2 ''The real issue is who is this impacting the most? It's the franchisees,'' said Keith Miller, a Subway franchisee in Northern California who has become an advocate for the interests of others like him. ''There was a lot of back-room dealing that made this happen and no time for anyone to really voice opposition.''\par \par \uc2 Willie Armstrong, the chief of staff for Assemblyman Chris Holden, a Democrat, who is the sponsor of A.B. 1228, said the lawmaker expected the measure to be approved by the Legislature before its session ended on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Legislature passed Assembly Bill 257, a measure Mr. Holden also sponsored, which would have created a council with the authority to raise the minimum wage to $22 per hour for restaurant workers. Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  signed it on Labor Day last year.\par \par \uc2 But the bill met fierce opposition from business interests and restaurant companies, and a petition received enough signatures to put a measure on the November 2024 ballot to stop the law from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 Other business groups in California have successfully used that tactic to change or reverse legislation they opposed.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, ride-sharing and delivery companies like \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Instacart\uc2  campaigned for and received an exemption from a key provision of Assembly Bill 5, which was signed by Mr. Newsom and would have made it much harder for the companies to classify drivers as independent contractors rather than employees.\par \par \uc2 Those companies collected enough signatures to get the issue on the ballot as Proposition 22, which passed in November 2020. More than $200 million was spent on that measure, making it the costliest ballot initiative in the state at the time.\par \par \uc2 And in February, oil companies received enough signatures for a measure that aims to block legislation banning new drilling projects near homes and schools. That initiative will be on the 2024 ballot.\par \par \uc2 In response to calls from advocacy groups who have said the referendum process unfairly benefits wealthy special-interest groups, and in an effort to demystify a system that many Californians say is confusing, Mr. Newsom signed legislation on Sept. 8 that aims to simplify the referendum process.\par \par \uc2 Kurtis Lee contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kurtis Lee contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 An agreement over regulations paved the way for the minimum wage in the industry to increase to $20 an hour. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MIKE BLAKE/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230913ej9d00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
