\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Contract Deadline Near, Union and Automakers Still Far Apart in Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neal E. Boudette \par \uc2 987 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  has said it is prepared to strike at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  if a deal is not reached before current contracts end on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  and the three established U.S. automakers remain far apart on wages and other issues with less than a week to go before contracts covering 150,000 union workers expire.\par \par \uc2 So far, the companies -- \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the parent of Chrysler -- have offered to raise pay by 14 percent to 16 percent over four years. Their offers include lump sum payments to help ease the impact of inflation, and policy changes that would lift the pay of recent hires and temporary workers, who typically earn about a third less than veteran union members.\par \par \uc2 But the union's combative new president, Shawn Fain, has dismissed the offers as ''insulting,'' noting that the three manufacturers have been making near-record profits for almost a decade, and that pay packages of top executives have increased substantially. He has been seeking pay increases of about 40 percent and repeatedly warned that workers were ready to leave assembly lines when the current collective bargaining agreements with the automakers expire on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fain has said the union is willing to strike at all three automakers simultaneously, a step it has never taken before. An across-the-board stoppage would deal a big blow to the economies of Michigan and other states.\par \par \uc2 ''We aren't going to stand by and allow them to drag out the negotiations like they've done in the past,'' Mr. Fain said Friday in a video on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . ''If we hit 11:59 on Thursday without a deal at any of the Big Three automakers, there will be a strike -- at all three if need be.''\par \par \uc2 The talks are taking place during a sweeping shift from combustion engine cars and trucks to electric vehicles, which require fewer parts and less labor to produce. U.A.W. leaders and members are increasingly worried that the transition will eliminate jobs and, over time, reduce wages and benefits.\par \par \uc2 The automakers are also worried about the transition. G.M., Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  are spending tens of billions of dollars to build new factories and scour the world for battery raw materials like lithium. Company executives have argued that offering the U.A.W. members big raises could leave them at a significant cost disadvantage to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which dominates the U.S. electric car market and employs nonunion workers.\par \par \uc2 The auto industry is the largest U.S. manufacturing sector, and accounts for about 3 percent of the nation's economic output. The three Detroit automakers operate dozens of plants that make about 500,000 cars a month.\par \par \uc2 The Anderson Economic Group, a research firm in East Lansing, Mich., estimated that a 10-day strike against the three companies would reduce the companies' profits by $1 billion and wages by $900 million for U.A.W. members and workers employed by other companies that depend on the automakers.\par \par \uc2 Aside from wages, the union and the companies remain far apart on several other matters, including measures to preserve jobs and discourage the closing of U.S. plants, increases in retirement benefits and cost-of-living adjustments, which were once standard in U.A.W. contracts.\par \par \uc2 The union has made some progress in its discussions with Ford. In response to Mr. Fain's demands, the automaker offered to increase wages by about 15 percent, through a 9 percent increase in base wages and one-time lump sum payments of $11,000 per worker. While Mr. Fain rejected that, the two sides have continued bargaining. He was scheduled to update U.A.W. members later on Friday about Ford's latest offer.\par \par \uc2 Talks with G.M. and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have proceeded more slowly. The U.A.W. filed a complaint last week with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , saying the two manufacturers had refused to offer proposals in response to the union's demands and were not negotiating in good faith.\par \par \uc2 G.M. responded by offering a combination of base wage increases and lump sum payments that would lift worker pay by about 16 percent. ''We have already said we want to reward and recognize our employees with wage increases,'' \uc2 Gerald Johnson\uc2 , G.M.'s executive vice president for global manufacturing, said this week.\par \par \uc2 Agreeing to all of the union's demands would threaten G.M.'s ability to compete, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Fain said the wage offer didn't go far enough to make up for the impact of inflation on workers' take-home pay over the last decade, and was too little in light of the profits G.M. was making. The automaker reported profits of $7 billion in the first half of the year. Mr. Fain also complained that G.M. had rejected the union's proposals on job security, retiree pay, cost-of-living adjustments and other issues.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  submitted its proposal to the union Friday morning, offering a 14.5 percent rise in base wages with no lump-sum payments.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a responsible and strong offer that positions us to continue providing good jobs to our employees,'' \uc2 Mark Stewart\uc2 , the chief operating officer of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's North American operations, said in a statement. ''With this offer, we are seeking a timely resolution to our discussions.''\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which is based in Amsterdam and was created by the merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  and \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  in 2021, earned 11 billion euros ($12 billion) in the first half of the year, a record.\par \par \uc2 United Auto Workers members marching in a Labor Day Parade in Detroit. A contract covering 150,000 union workers will expire on Thursday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BILL PUGLIANO/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230909ej9900046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
