\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Kering's Chief Strikes Deal For the Talent Agency \uc2 CAA\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Hirsch and Vanessa Friedman \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal for \uc2 Creative Artists Agency\uc2  by \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois-Henri Pinault\uc2 's family office reflects the growing convergence of celebrities and fashion.\par \par \uc2 In a deal that shows the ever-growing convergence of fashion and fame, \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois-Henri Pinault\uc2 , the French billionaire and chief executive of the luxury goods company Kering, said on Thursday that his family office had bought a majority stake in one of Hollywood's biggest talent agencies, \uc2 Creative Artists Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pinault's family office, \uc2 Art\u233\'00\'E9mis\uc2 , already has investments that include the auction house Christie's and a stake in \uc2 Puma\uc2 . Like them, \uc2 CAA\uc2  will be managed separately from Kering, which owns such brands as Gucci, Saint Laurent and Balenciaga.\par \par \uc2 Talent -- actors, singers, filmmakers -- has become a uniquely powerful tool in selling products. Celebrities, be they actors, athletes or social media stars, give a brand direct access to legions of followers and are handsomely paid for that access. Fashion brands are increasingly racing to lock in relationships with the next big name.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no separation between fashion and entertainment anymore,'' said Robert Burke, the eponymous founder of a luxury consultancy, calling the deal a ''natural, if unprecedented, evolution.''\par \par \uc2 CAA\uc2 's clients include some of the biggest celebrities: Tom Hanks, \uc2 Steven Spielberg\uc2  and Zendaya -- as well as Mr. Pinault's wife, the actress Salma Hayek Pinault, who is often next to her husband in the front row of fashion shows, and who often wears Gucci and Balenciaga gowns on the red carpet.\par \par \uc2 The deal offers \uc2 Art\u233\'00\'E9mis\uc2  ''increased diversity, both in terms of geographical footprint and business activities, to our other assets,'' Mr. Pinault said in a statement. ''\uc2 CAA\uc2 's exceptional insight, relationships and access across key sectors, combined with their widely regarded level of collaboration and innovation, gives the company a formidable role in driving global opportunities.''\par \par \uc2 CAA\uc2  has been led by the powerhouse team of \uc2 Bryan Lourd\uc2 , Kevin Huvane and Richard Lovett, and they will remain as chairmen, according to a news release about the deal. Mr. Lourd will be named chief executive when the deal closes.\par \par \uc2 Along with the appointment of the music producer Pharrell Williams as creative director of Louis Vuitton men's wear, the \uc2 CAA\uc2  acquisition reflects just how important access to celebrity has become to the fashion world -- and how important fashion is to celebrities. Especially during the actors' strike, as commercial work with brands remains one of the few avenues of income and profile-raising that the union, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , approves.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the private equity firm \uc2 TPG\uc2  took a 35 percent stake in \uc2 CAA\uc2 , then broadened its hold on the talent agency to 53 percent four years later. \uc2 Art\u233\'00\'E9mis\uc2  is acquiring that majority stake, according to the news release, though it did not specify the price.\par \par \uc2 The agency's rivals, meanwhile, have raced to scale up to strengthen their negotiating power with streaming powerhouses like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 United Talent Agency\uc2 , which represents celebrities like Chris Pratt, Bad Bunny and Lizzo, bought the literary agency Fletcher & Company in January after acquiring the London-based Curtis Brown agency last year. The \uc2 Endeavor Group\uc2 , a \uc2 CAA\uc2  rival, acquired full control of the wrestling business Ultimate Fighting Championship in 2021 ahead of \uc2 Endeavor\uc2 's initial public offering. This year, \uc2 Endeavor\uc2  struck a deal to merge UFC with World Wide Wrestling as part of a new company.\par \par \uc2 As these talent agencies grow -- and further intertwine themselves with fashion -- new questions may emerge about the nature of these relationships. Even if the new \uc2 CAA\uc2  is separate from Kering, for example, would \uc2 LVMH\uc2 , the world's dominant luxury group, want to pay millions to celebrities to represent its brands when it knows that a percentage of that payment will enrich a company with a relationship to its competitor?\par \par \uc2 After all, ''even if it's managed separately, this offers enormous access to talent,'' Mr. Burke said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 CAA\uc2  acquisition is the latest in a series of moves from Mr. Pinault. This year, Kering bought a 30 percent stake in Valentino, the Roman fashion brand and red carpet staple, with an option to acquire the whole thing by 2028. That deal followed the Kering purchase of Creed, the high-end fragrance brand, for $3.83 billion.\par \par \uc2 And Kering itself has also ventured into film. Saint Laurent became the first luxury fashion brand with a movie subsidiary when it introduced Saint Laurent Productions in April; its debut short, directed by Pedro Almod\u243\'00\'F3var, premiered at the Cannes Film Festival in May. (Kering became an official partner of the film festival in 2015.)\par \par \uc2 As to whether other luxury moguls might follow Mr. Pinault's lead and look to acquire their own talent agency, Mr. Burke added, ''I'm never surprised by anyone in fashion following someone else's smart idea.''\par \par \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7ois-Henri Pinault's move links the worlds of fashion and celebrity. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LAUREN FLEISHMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); The actress and singer Zendaya is among the celebrity clients represented by Creative Artists Agency, one of Hollywood's biggest talent agencies. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEVE MARCUS/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230908ej980003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
