\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 West Coast Dockworkers Ratify 6-Year Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The six-year agreement is expected to increase traffic at Pacific ports, which had sagged because of the prospect of a walkout.\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers at ports along the West Coast have ratified a new contract, securing a sweeping agreement set to last six years and expected to ease tensions after cargo shipments were diverted to other regions.\par \par \uc2 The contract between the \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2  and the \uc2 Pacific Maritime Association\uc2 , which operates the terminals, covers 22,000 dockworkers at 29 ports from Los Angeles to Seattle.\par \par \uc2 The contract was approved by 75 percent of members who voted, the union said late Thursday. Details of the agreement were not released publicly, and the union declined to comment. Unionized workers at the ports have average salaries in the low six figures.\par \par \uc2 The maritime association did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The two sides announced in June that they had reached a tentative agreement after a year of negotiations that prompted intervention from the Biden administration and coincided with a decline in the volume of cargo at several major ports along the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 During the negotiation period, as workers staged a series of slowdowns, including at the twin ports of Los Angeles and Long Beach, some shipping companies diverted freight to ports along the Gulf and East Coasts and then never returned to their old routes.\par \par \uc2 And the movement of goods continued to lag into the summer.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2 , the amount of cargo imported in July was down 25 percent from a year earlier. But at Port Houston, where some companies rerouted cargo, officials reported its best July on record in processing cargo.\par \par \uc2 Geraldine Knatz, a former head of the \uc2 Port of Los Angeles\uc2  and now professor of the practice of policy and engineering at the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 , said she expected the contract's ratification to give some shippers the level of comfort they needed to return to their old routes.\par \par \uc2 ''Everyone is expecting we will see an increase in volume,'' she said of cargo handled on the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 Matthew Shay\uc2 , president of the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 , said the West Coast ports played a critical role in the vitality of the business community nationwide.\par \par \uc2 ''Now that an agreement has been ratified by all parties, the millions of businesses and employees who rely on their operations can be assured that long-term stability will remain at the West Coast ports,'' Mr. Shay said.\par \par \uc2 Santul Nerkar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Santul Nerkar contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 At the Port of Los Angeles, the amount of cargo imported in July was down 25 percent from a year earlier, but Port Houston reported its best July on record. (PHOTOGRAPH BY COLEY BROWN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230902ej920003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
