\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ripples of Hollywood Strikes Affect Other Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lydia DePillis \par \uc2 836 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walkouts by screenwriters and actors have meant less work in fields that cater to the TV and film industry.\par \par \uc2 One reason the August employment report wasn't stronger: Television and movie production has largely halted since a deadlock in contract negotiations between major studios and unions that represent screenwriters and actors.\par \par \uc2 The motion picture and sound recording industry subtracted 16,800 jobs in August. That's not a huge share of its approximately 438,000-person work force, but it underestimates the total impact of the labor stoppages, given how much spending power the film industry creates in Los Angeles specifically.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown started when 11,500 members of the Writers Guild of America went on strike in May. In the second quarter alone, according to Los Angeles's film office, activity was down 28.8 percent from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The stoppages spread when \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , which represents more than 160,000 actors and broadcasters, struck in July after its contract with the largest film and television studios expired.\par \par \uc2 Striking actors and writers, however, don't translate one for one into payrolls. For one thing, many of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 's members work for television news stations and aren't on strike. Those who do act in movies and TV shows usually sign contracts, sometimes for a day or a week, rather than entering into a continuing employment relationship.\par \par \uc2 Between intermittent gigs, they're used to taking second jobs, like waiting on tables or designing websites. During the strike, they're also allowed to work in theater and commercials, as well as on a handful of independent projects that have agreed to abide by the union's demands.\par \par \uc2 Even with no work, most earn at least some money through residuals -- although that revenue has shrunk with the rise of streaming, and will fade as the months drag on.\par \par \uc2 ''We're used to being freelancers, and just being able to go along,'' said Jodi Long, president of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 's Los Angeles local. ''For now, what's really going to affect the job market is the people on set -- the hair and makeup people, the gaffers and the grips and the people in production.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Long is right: The support services required to make movies and shows have largely shut down. Some serve other industries as well, but many have grown up around the needs of film production. Even if the industry becomes very busy when the strike ends as studios restock their pipelines, months of income will be hard to replace.\par \par \uc2 Take Limelight Catering. Its owner, Steve Michelson, mostly mothballed the business in May when the writers' strike started, laying off 50 staff members, nearly all of them represented by the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 . Since then, he has been repairing trucks and doing other maintenance at his facility in the northern reaches of the Los Angeles area.\par \par \uc2 ''We're kind of the side effect,'' Mr. Michelson said. ''We depend on the film industry, but we get nothing out of this. The actors and the writers, hopefully they'll get a nice raise, but we get nothing out of it.''\par \par \uc2 Unlike striking workers in California, those who lose their jobs as collateral damage of labor disputes are eligible for unemployment insurance. (New York State does allow workers on strike to collect unemployment checks.)\par \par \uc2 That's what most of Mr. Michelson's workers are doing. Many of those who were in more physical jobs, like carrying heavy cameras and lights around, are using the time to take care of occupational injuries by claiming disability benefits.\par \par \uc2 Bill Bridges, a member of the International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees, has worked as a grip for 25 years. Getting through the Covid-19 shutdown was hard enough, he said, and then he needed a year off for a total knee replacement. During that time, Mr. Bridges became licensed to drive a truck, and applied for jobs with the long-haul freight lines -- but he said they paid only $650 a week for someone with no experience.\par \par \uc2 After recovering from surgery, he was able to drive film trucks, and sometimes earned $1,600 a day. That stopped when the talent went on strike. This time, he's back on disability to get bunion surgery.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bridges supports the strikers, but said he was way behind on bills, barely sustaining his wife and 11-year-old son. The union has started a mutual aid food pantry and a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  appeal for its members.\par \par \uc2 ''This is probably financially the lowest point in my life,'' he said. He worries about his own union's contract negotiations, coming up next year: ''If there's another strike, I don't know what I'm going to do.''\par \par \uc2 The SAG-AFTRA union has been on strike since July. The support services required to make movies have also largely shut down. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JENNA SCHOENEFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230902ej920003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
