\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Job Openings Fell in July as Labor Market Cooled\b0\par\par\uc2 By J. Edward Moreno \par \uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 The number of job openings continued to drop in July, the Labor Department reported Tuesday, another sign that the U.S. labor market is losing its momentum.\par \par \uc2 There were 8.8 million job openings last month, down from about 9.2 million in June and the lowest level since March 2021, according to the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey. The amount of people quitting their jobs, a measure of workers' confidence in the job market, continued to nudge down in July as well.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: Implications for interest-rate policy.\par \par \uc2 Labor market data is closely watched by policymakers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as they combat stubborn inflation.\par \par \uc2 ''For workers, this looks like fewer opportunities -- if you leave your job now, you're less likely to land a better one than you were last year at this time,'' Elizabeth Renter, a data analyst at the personal finance site \uc2 NerdWallet\uc2 , said in an email statement. ''For the Fed, this likely looks according to plan.''\par \par \uc2 Fed policymakers lifted interest rates to a range of 5.25 to 5.5 percent in their last meeting in July, the highest since 2001. Only one Fed meeting has passed since March 2022 where the central bank has not raised rates. Some investors hope that signs the labor market is continuing to cool will push the Fed to end its campaign of rate increases sooner.\par \par \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , signaled on Friday that the central bank was not ruling out more rate increases.\par \par \uc2 ''We are prepared to raise rates further if appropriate, and intend to hold policy at a restrictive level until we are confident that inflation is moving sustainably down toward our objective,'' Mr. Powell said at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City\uc2 's annual Jackson Hole conference in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 The new data is likely to be welcomed by the Fed, said Layla O'Kane, a senior economist at Lightcast, a labor market analytics firm. It shows that what the Fed has been doing is working, but policymakers are not likely to declare their mission accomplished just yet, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a really good sign for a cooling labor market, but it's not a cool labor market yet,'' Ms. O'Kane said. ''There's some way to go before we think we solved some of the labor market tightness.''\par \par \uc2 Background: A surprisingly robust labor market.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. labor market has defied expectations by remaining strong despite the Fed's mission to slow down the economy by raising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Consistently strong labor data initially fueled predictions that the Fed would continue rate increases until the economy fell into a recession. Many have taken a more optimistic view recently as inflation has begun to moderate alongside a strong labor market.\par \par \uc2 Employers are starting to feel the effects of high interest rates, said \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 . Companies are being more judicious in their hiring even if they need more people, in part because of the high cost of labor, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''With interest rates this high, some investments don't pencil out,'' Ms. Pollak said. ''Businesses that would have opened another location or invested in another truck or another warehouse are taking it slow.''\par \par \uc2 What's Next: The August jobs report on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The August employment report will be released by the Labor Department on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate dropped to 3.5 percent in July, a sign that although the labor market is cooling, workers are generally still able to find opportunities. The unemployment data for August will be one of the last labor market pulses that Fed policymakers will get before their next meeting on Sept. 19-20.\par \par \uc2 A job fair in Minneapolis last month. There were 8.8 million job openings in July, down from about 9.2 million in June and the lowest level since March 2021. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TIM GRUBER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230830ej8u0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
