\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Union's Actions in Georgia Aim to Raise the Heat On Biden and Automakers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Weisman \par \uc2 1,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A coalition of unions and civic groups is pushing one of the world's largest automakers to protect and train workers in return for federal money under President Biden's signature laws.\par \par \uc2 A coalition of labor unions and civic groups in Georgia and Alabama will launch a pressure campaign on Monday targeting Hyundai's electric vehicle plants and its clean energy suppliers, an effort that could also push the Biden administration to make good on its oft-repeated pledge to create not just jobs but ''good union jobs.''\par \par \uc2 By focusing on the shift to electric vehicles at Hyundai, a nonunion carmaker expected to reap huge benefits from Mr. Biden's prized initiatives, the coalition hopes to make inroads at other automakers, such as B.M.W. in South Carolina and \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  in Alabama, which similarly chose union-hostile territory for their American manufacturing bases.\par \par \uc2 The campaign could also raise the heat on domestic automakers in the middle of contract negotiations with the newly aggressive \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 , which is focused on raising wages at electric vehicle suppliers like battery makers.\par \par \uc2 For Mr. Biden, the Hyundai campaign has political ramifications, in setting specific demands on one of the largest automakers in the world in one of the most important swing states in the 2024 presidential election, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 ''The people in the community should be able to come to work in these plants, with a livable wage and good jobs,'' said Yvonne T. Brooks, president of the Georgia State A.F.L.-C.I.O., adding that ''to bring jobs here but not provide a livable wage kind of defeats the purpose.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has campaigned on the sheer number of jobs created by his three signature laws, a $1 trillion infrastructure package, a $280 billion measure to rekindle a domestic semiconductor industry, and the Inflation Reduction Act, which included $370 billion for clean energy to combat climate change. A $25 million advertising blitz announced by his campaign last week kicked off with a one-minute spot that proclaims, ''Manufacturing jobs are coming home,'' and ''America is leading the world in clean energy.''\par \par \uc2 But despite low unemployment, tempering inflation and steady job creation, Mr. Biden's overall approval ratings have been dragged down by voters' refusal to give him credit for the good economic news. Clifford Young, who oversees U.S. public opinion research at \uc2 Ipsos\uc2 , a polling company, said that last year's 8.5 percent inflation and the ensuing interest rate hikes and slower economic growth might have sealed Mr. Biden's fate with the voting public.\par \par \uc2 ''The dirty secret is a bad economy hurts a president more than a good economy helps,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 White House officials, who were notified of the Hyundai effort ahead of time, said Thursday that Mr. Biden fully backs the aims of the coalition in Georgia. And labor leaders have generally supported Mr. Biden as the most pro-union president ever.\par \par \uc2 But in a notable shift, those leaders also say the volume of jobs created on his watch may not be enough to win worker loyalties if those jobs are low-paid, dangerous and insecure. That is especially true if substandard jobs are underwritten by the taxpayer.\par \par \uc2 ''I know the president can't make stipulations that all new jobs have to go to union workers, but there have to be fair labor standards for jobs that are supported by tax dollars,'' said David Green, the \uc2 United Automobile Workers\uc2 ' regional director for Ohio and Indiana. ''Members are a little frustrated with it. It's our tax dollars, too.''\par \par \uc2 Such concerns have led the U.A.W. to withhold its endorsement of Mr. Biden as the union's new leadership threatens to strike over wages and benefits at electric vehicle suppliers. Mr. Biden has responded with support, tapping a senior adviser and Democratic veteran, Gene B. Sperling, as liaison between the union and the automakers and backing the U.A.W. this month in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 But union leaders are worried about the transition that Mr. Biden has set in motion with his push to address climate change with federal money funding the shift from fossil fuels. They are pressing automakers shifting to electric vehicles to ''honor the right to organize,'' take necessary steps to avoid plant closings, and provide training programs to help workers transition into new jobs at comparable wages.\par \par \uc2 A letter to the chief executive of Hyundai's American subsidiary, \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Mu\u241\'00\'F1oz\uc2 , signed by coalition members including the U.A.W., the A.F.L.-C.I.O., the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers\uc2  (which is particularly close to Mr. Biden), and religious, community and environmental groups, maps key labor demands.\par \par \uc2 Such letters, demanding ''community benefits agreements'' enforced with binding arbitration systems, have been trotted out in the past to little effect. But union leaders said the Hyundai effort is more focused and forward-looking, hinting at the strategy of organizers in the South as unions across the country have become much more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 The letter pushes for Hyundai and its subsidiaries to hire locally, train workers from the communities around the plants, bolster safety standards, and protect the environment around the plants, which are expected to employ more than 30,000 Georgians and Alabamians. Of those, 12,750 are expected to work at or around Hyundai's new electric vehicle ''megasite'' in Bryan County near Savannah, the largest economic development project in Georgia's history.\par \par \uc2 The coalition is seeking a binding agreement modeled on one reached last year with the electric bus maker New Flyer, which promised, among other things, that at least 45 percent of new hires and 20 percent of promotions would be women, minorities and U.S. military veterans.\par \par \uc2 ''These facilities will transform our communities, and we are faced with a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to ensure that this transformation is for the best,'' the coalition wrote, demanding that Hyundai and its suppliers come to the bargaining table to make ''highroad commitments to workers and their communities.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Hyundai U.S.A., Michael Stewart, said in a statement that the company's ''top priority is the safety and well-being of the more than 114,000 individuals we employ, directly and indirectly, whose market-leading skills and expertise are driving America's auto industry forward.''\par \par \uc2 Daniel Flippo, director of the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  southeast district, cautioned that community agreements might not have the teeth of union contracts.\par \par \uc2 At a recent meeting with Energy Department officials about the electric vehicle transition, Mr. Flippo said, he told them, ''Look, all this going in to protect workers and worker rights looks good on paper, but if you don't follow up, it shouldn't be up to the union organizers to do it for you.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats secured a raft of provisions in Mr. Biden's three signature laws to encourage labor organizing, raise wages and favor union apprenticeships and training programs. In May, the administration used those provisions to apply pressure to a Georgia electric bus company, \uc2 Blue Bird\uc2 , and support workers trying to unionize its Fort Valley, Ga., plant. The \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2  won that vote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Flippo credited a rule in the electric school bus grants that said no federal money could be used to oppose union organizing, such as in hiring union-busting law firms during an organizing drive.\par \par \uc2 ''They did use some of those tactics,'' he said, ''but all we had to say was we were going to notify the government and request an audit of where their money was going, and it went away.''\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has had some successes with clean energy companies, securing commitments by a Danish wind energy giant to use union labor on its offshore wind projects and by a North Dakota metals company to stay neutral in any union drive at its new battery plant.\par \par \uc2 But union leaders have only so much influence over the rank and file -- and against the pull of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who has made working-class appeals central to his political movement. Mr. Green pointed to the former president's promise to revive a \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant in \uc2 Lordstown\uc2 , Ohio, by enticing an untested start-up to buy the facility. That start-up, \uc2 Lordstown Motors\uc2 , filed for bankruptcy protection in late June.\par \par \uc2 ''I will not, could not support any endorsement of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,'' he said. ''But we've got a lot of members. Do I think that Trump's rhetoric is contagious among our members? Absolutely.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230828ej8s0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
