\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Florida 'Effectively Banned' A.P. Psychology, College Board Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Mervosh \par \uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The nonprofit said it would not remove a section on gender and sexual orientation, as Florida had requested, and advised districts not to offer the course.\par \par \uc2 The College Board announced on Thursday that Florida school districts should no longer offer Advanced Placement Psychology, one of the most popular A.P. courses, the latest skirmish in its battle with the state's Department of Education over how to teach race, gender and sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 The College Board, the nonprofit that oversees advanced placement courses and the SAT, revoked its support for A.P. Psychology in Florida, saying it would not abide by the state's demand to remove a longstanding section on gender and sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 Florida Department of Education\uc2  has effectively banned A.P. Psychology in the state,'' the College Board said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Education fired back, accusing the College Board of ''playing games with Florida students'' one week before school starts.\par \par \uc2 ''The Department didn't 'ban' the course,'' the department said in a statement. ''The course remains listed in Florida's Course Code Directory for the 2023-24 school year. We encourage the College Board to stop playing games with Florida students and continue to offer the course and allow teachers to operate accordingly.''\par \par \uc2 Under an expanded Florida rule, instruction on gender identity and sexual orientation is now restricted in most cases through the 12th grade. The \uc2 Florida Department of Education\uc2  had asked the College Board and other providers of advanced, college-level courses to search their offerings for potential violations.\par \par \uc2 But the College Board said that it would not modify its content, and that any course that did not address gender and sexual orientation should not be labeled ''advanced placement.''\par \par \uc2 ''To be clear, any A.P. Psychology course taught in Florida will violate either Florida law or college requirements,'' the College Board said.\par \par \uc2 The College Board, a powerful nonprofit, has been waging war with the administration of Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, since earlier this year when his administration rejected the College Board's new African American studies course. The curriculum included topics such as ''queer studies,'' reparations and the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 , and the administration objected, citing a state law limiting how racism and other aspects of history are taught in public schools.\par \par \uc2 The battle exposed the College Board's negotiations with the DeSantis administration, and its changes to the curriculum.\par \par \uc2 But the fight over A.P. Psychology moves the battlefield from a new course that was taking feedback and being piloted, to long-established academic territory.\par \par \uc2 A.P. Psychology has been around for three decades, and it has included a section on gender and sexual orientation as part of the framework since the course's inception, the College Board said. The section comes as part of a unit on developmental psychology, spanning childhood and adolescence to older adulthood, with themes on ''moral development'' as well as on gender and sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 ''Describe how sex and gender influence socialization and other aspects of development,'' the College Board's framework for that segment says.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Psychological Association\uc2  has supported the inclusion of gender identity and sexual orientation as a necessary part of studying human development.\par \par \uc2 ''An advanced placement course that ignores the decades of science studying sexual orientation and gender identity would deprive students of knowledge they will need to succeed in their studies, in high school and beyond,'' the group's chief executive, Arthur C. Evans Jr., said in a statement Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In refusing to modify its course, the College Board said in June that it had ''learned from our mistakes'' in the rollout of the A.P. African American Studies course and asserted that ''we must be clear from the outset where we stand.''\par \par \uc2 The latest developments leave school districts scrambling just days before the school year is scheduled to start next week for some districts.\par \par \uc2 More than 28,000 students in Florida took the A.P. Psychology course last year, and the class can result in college credit for some students who score high enough on an end-of-course exam.\par \par \uc2 Florida's Board of Education meeting in July to discuss adopting new rules required by state law. (PHOTOGRAPH BY WILLIE J. ALLEN JR./ORLANDO SENTINEL, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230804ej840003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
