\par\par \uc2 TIMES INSIDER\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Actors' Strike and Hollywood's Future, in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Josh Ocampo \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lights. Camera. Action? Brooks Barnes, who covers the entertainment business, discussed the state of film and television amid an industrywide shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Times Insider explains who we are and what we do and delivers behind-the-scenes insights into how our journalism comes together.\par \par \uc2 It was around 1 a.m. one Thursday last month when Brooks Barnes received the email he'd been waiting up for.\par \par \uc2 ''SAG-AFTRA TELEVISION, THEATRICAL AND STREAMING CONTRACTS EXPIRE WITHOUT A DEAL,'' read the subject line on the email, sent by a union representative.\par \par \uc2 Movie studios and unionized actors failed to reach a deal after weeks of negotiations. Hours later, members of \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 's national board voted to strike, and tens of thousands of actors joined the screenwriters already on the picket lines over issues including pay. The decision brought film and television productions to a standstill and left the fate of Hollywood hanging in the balance.\par \par \uc2 ''When something big like this happens, you just have to put down everything else you're working on,'' said Mr. Barnes, a reporter who covers Hollywood for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an interview, he shared his thoughts on Hollywood's first industrywide shutdown in more than 60 years and on how the repercussions may be coming to a theater near you. This interview has been edited.\par \par \uc2 What do unionized actors want?\par \par \uc2 There's a long list of things; their proposals are detailed and specific, down to what a background dancer gets paid for rehearsal time, for example. But the main sticking point is that actors want residual payments from streaming services.\par \par \uc2 In the traditional model, actors would get paid for the work that they do on a TV show or movie; they would get paid residuals once that show or movie was resold as a rerun on TV. Sometimes the residual money could be huge, depending on a show's popularity.\par \par \uc2 In the streaming era, that model has changed. Actors still get paid a residual for streaming work. But it's essentially a flat fee. Actors want those payments to be based on a show's popularity -- more for a hit like ''Stranger Things,'' for example, and less for something that flops.\par \par \uc2 The other big sticking point is artificial intelligence. Actors want guardrails so their likenesses will not be reused digitally without their approval and a payment.\par \par \uc2 Using an actor's likeness without their consent makes me think of a recent ''Black Mirror'' episode, in which characters' likenesses were used in bizarre ways without their permission.\par \par \uc2 That's exactly what this is about, but it's also to protect background actors. In a crowd scene, they might scan a background actor's likeness and reuse it in another movie just to populate the scene. It doesn't have to be Salma Hayek or Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 How does the writers' strike fit into all this?\par \par \uc2 The writers are on strike for similar issues, including residual payments. Writers are also looking for a type of quota system; they want studios to staff a writers' room with a minimum number of writers. Streaming services often use minirooms, a type of writers' room used early in the show-development process that involves half as many writers. Basically, they're doing much of the same work with fewer people. The union wants protections against those job cuts.\par \par \uc2 How soon will we see the repercussions of the actors' strike?\par \par \uc2 Viewers won't see too many repercussions for a while because the assembly pipelines work so far in advance; a lot of upcoming TV series and films are already finished. But some big movies planned for Christmas have been pushed to next year, and the fall TV schedule will be heavy on reality shows and reruns. Actors are also not allowed to promote any of the work that they have already finished. And that's crucial to studios; they want actors on talk shows and podcasts to promote their projects.\par \par \uc2 You recently wrote about a factor that's contributing to the strikes: the absence of a power broker to help mediate.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the last Hollywood strike took place in 2007-8. In those days, it was a simpler business; \uc2 Netflix\uc2  was mostly an indie company and had just begun streaming. Back then, there were studio elders and senior statesmen who could come in and say, OK, let's iron this out and get back to work. That kind of person doesn't exist so much anymore.\par \par \uc2 Why not?\par \par \uc2 Companies just have different cultures and priorities -- a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  versus a \uc2 Disney\uc2  versus an \uc2 Apple\uc2 . The other reason is some of the studio executives who could mediate have had problems. \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 's chief executive, has become a bit of a villain for comments he made about the strike on CNBC, so he's not really the greatest person to generate trust. You need someone whom both sides trust, respect and will listen to.\par \par \uc2 I wonder about your thoughts on the success of ''Barbenheimer'' at the box office. It feels bittersweet.\par \par \uc2 It's exciting to know that Hollywood can still deliver these kinds of cultural thunderclaps, but the reality is the reality: The hits are few and far between. And it's hard to feel very good about the business when hundreds of thousands of people are on strike or impacted by the strikes.\par \par \uc2 SAG-AFTRA members went on strike last month after a unanimous vote, joining screenwriters on the picket lines and shutting down Hollywood. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARK ABRAMSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230802ej820004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
