\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Less Job Turnover Signals Cooling Labor Market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 Job turnover decreased in June, the Labor Department reported on Tuesday, suggesting that the American labor market continues to slow down from its meteoric ascent after the pandemic lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 The Numbers\par \par \uc2 There were 9.6 million job openings in June, roughly the same as a month earlier, according to the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey (JOLTS).\par \par \uc2 Employers have tightened the screws on hiring in recent months, with job openings falling to their lowest level since April 2021 as the economy responds to tightening monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The most notable changes in June were not in job openings but in hiring and quitting. There were 5.9 million hires in June, down from 6.2 million in May. And the quits rate, a measure of workers' confidence in the job market and bargaining power, decreased to 2.4 percent, from 2.6 percent in May and down from a record of 3 percent in April 2022.\par \par \uc2 The number of workers laid off was 1.5 million, about the same as in May.\par \par \uc2 Quotable: 'The labor market is unbalanced.'\par \par \uc2 ''We're still in an economy where the labor market is unbalanced,'' said \uc2 Michael Strain\uc2 , an economist at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , ''with the demand for workers substantially outpacing the supply of workers.'' There are roughly 1.6 job openings for each unemployed worker.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: The economy moves closer to a 'soft landing.'\par \par \uc2 Over the past 16 months, as they have sought to curb inflation and make sure the economy does not overheat, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policymakers have pursued the coveted ''soft landing.'' That means bringing down inflation to the Fed's target of 2 percent by raising interest rates without causing a significant jump in unemployment, avoiding a recession.\par \par \uc2 The June JOLTS report provides more optimism that the Fed is approaching that soft landing, as demand for workers remains robust while tapering gradually. Inflation remains high by historical standards -- at 3 percent, according to the latest data -- but has eased substantially.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a really strong labor market that is staying strong but slowing down,'' said Preston Mui, a senior economist at Employ America, a research and advocacy group focused on the job market.\par \par \uc2 At the end of their meeting last Wednesday, policymakers raised rates a quarter-point, and the Fed's chair, \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , said its staff economists were no longer projecting a recession for 2023. But Mr. Powell left the door open to further rate increases and said the economy still had ''a long way to go'' to 2 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Background: It's been a good time to be a worker.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. economy rapidly rose out of the Covid-19 recession in 2020, a powerful narrative built: ''Nobody wants to work.'' There was some truth to that hyperbole. Employers had a hard time finding workers, and workers reaped the rewards, quitting their jobs to find better-paying ones (and succeeding).\par \par \uc2 With quit rates falling in recent months, the so-called great resignation appears to be over, if not receding, and the continued downward trajectory of job openings implies that employers are less eager to fill staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 Employers are not hiring with the fervor they were a few months ago, but they are not yet casting aside workers, who might not lose the gains they have achieved during the pandemic recovery.\par \par \uc2 What's Next: The July jobs report lands on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department will release the July employment report on Friday. The unemployment rate for June sat at 3.6 percent, a dip from 3.7 percent in May but higher than the 3.4 percent recorded in January and April, the lowest jobless rate since 1969.\par \par \uc2 June was the 30th consecutive month of gains in U.S. payrolls, as the economy added 209,000 jobs, and economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  expected the economy to have added another 200,000 jobs in July. Fed policymakers will be watching the report closely, but one more month's data will arrive before they next convene Sept. 19-20.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230802ej820003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
