\par\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 For Two Empty Nesters, Would $300,000 Be Enough for a New House in Indiana?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitch Smith \par \uc2 847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the pandemic waned, Selene Carter and Steven Hendren came to a crossroads. The couple, who married in 2015, had been renting a cramped house near downtown Bloomington, Ind., for years. It was within walking distance of their jobs at \uc2 Indiana University Bloomington\uc2 , and they had raised their blended family of three children there. But as the children grew to be college age, empty-nest syndrome set in.\par \par \uc2 The couple, both in their 50s, liked living close to coffee shops and appreciated the progressive ethos of Bloomington, a college town nestled in wooded hills about an hour south of Indianapolis. But now, renting near campus made them feel almost as if they were back in graduate school.\par \par \uc2 Working from home during Covid had been a clear sign that it was time to move. ''He had his computer in the living room, and we didn't have a dining room -- we had no counter space,'' said Ms. Carter, an associate professor of dance, who struggled to lead Zoom classes that required moving around. ''For being in our 50s, it just didn't fit our lifestyle.''\par \par \uc2 They entered a competitive housing market last year with high hopes, but limited funds for a down payment. The goal was a house on the outskirts of Bloomington with enough space for both of them to work from home, but not too far from campus. They wanted extra bedrooms for visitors, and more than two bathrooms. Ms. Carter wanted a bigger kitchen. Mr. Hendren, who works in the university's library department, hoped to find some space for woodworking projects.\par \par \uc2 Their broker, John Saunders, said there were options in the couple's price range, which initially went up to about $310,000. But they were unable to put much down, making them less attractive to some sellers in a frenzied market where cash buyers were winning bidding wars.\par \par \uc2 Among the properties they considered:\par \par \uc2 1. Three-Bedroom With Big Yard\par \par \uc2 This three-bedroom, one-bathroom house was just north of Bloomington in a desirable neighborhood with scenic views. It had shiny wood floors and a spacious yard with a patio out back, although it lacked the kind of kitchen Ms. Carter wanted. And with 1,040 square feet (not including the unfinished basement), it wasn't ideal for hosting guests. The asking price was $274,900, with about $1,200 in annual taxes.\par \par \uc2 2. Large Kitchen on Historic Street\par \par \uc2 This brick ranch house was in a historic district with an exurban feel. The house had 2,175 square feet of living space, with four bedrooms, two and a half bathrooms, a fenced yard, a renovated eat-in kitchen, and a bedroom and bathroom in the basement. At $349,000, with almost $1,600 in annual taxes, it was above their budget.\par \par \uc2 3. Restored Ranch With Home Theater\par \par \uc2 This restored limestone ranch, just northwest of Bloomington, had four bedrooms and two bathrooms, with 2,155 finished square feet and some unfinished space. The basement had a screening room with theater-style seats. The large yard needed work, and there was a carport rather than a garage. But the house had nice modern finishes, and the kitchen, while tight, had stainless steel appliances. The price was $299,900, with about $610 in annual taxes.\par \par \uc2 Their Choice\par \par \uc2 Ms. Carter and Mr. Hendren made an early bid on the three-bedroom house, overlooking its unfinished basement and limited space for guests. They offered the asking price with an escalation clause up to $296,000, but lost out to another buyer.\par \par \uc2 Not long after, they fell in love with the restored ranch house. The yard needed work and the kitchen wasn't ideal, but there was ample room for their children to visit. They offered $30,000 over the asking price, but again were outbid.\par \par \uc2 Months passed, and the outlook seemed bleak. Having given their landlord notice, the couple fretted that they would miss their deadline to buy a place.\par \par \uc2 Then they spotted the brick ranch on the historic street. The kitchen looked ideal for entertaining. There were enough bedrooms to fashion two home offices. There was even a workspace in the garage for Mr. Hendren.\par \par \uc2 ''I felt like it could hold us and our interests really well,'' Mr. Hendren said. ''And then it turned out to be that way.''\par \par \uc2 The price was a sticking point, until a few relatives offered to help with a down payment. The couple ultimately paid $344,000, just below the asking price, with 5 percent down, and moved in last summer.\par \par \uc2 With extra room, they've been able to take old possessions out of storage. ''We put up these photos of our families,'' Ms. Carter said. ''It feels like we're kind of finally integrating our lives.''\par \par \uc2 Email thehunt@nytimes.com\par \par \uc2 The Buyers: Steven Hendren and Selene Carter in Bloomington, Ind. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KAITI SULLIVAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The Options This article appeared in print on page RE4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230730ej7u00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
